# base/tiers/regroup -- the two GUARD SHAPES agree, at every width.
#
# R3 states a set guard INTERLEAVED, `forall x. (set(x) -> (forall y. (set(y) ->
# B)))`. Several hand-written declarations here state it GROUPED instead,
# `forall x. forall y. (set(x) -> (set(y) -> B))`, and `GROUPED_IN_BASE` pins
# those. The two are equivalent as PROPOSITIONS and distinct as PROOF OBJECTS --
# they are eliminated in different orders -- so a kernel stated one way cannot be
# fed the other. This tier is the bridge, and it is why `base/` never has to move:
# the CONSUMER adapts.
#
# Proved from forall/implies/iff alone. No set theory, `B` uninterpreted, so R2
# holds parametrically and nothing is admitted.
#
# GENERATED by `class::regroup::tier()` through width 5; above that the walk
# generates on demand. Regenerate with `REGEN_REGROUP=1 cargo test
# the_regroup_tier_is_what_the_generator_emits`. Do not hand-edit.
schema regroup2 [B(2)]: ((forall x1. (forall x2. (set(x1) -> (set(x2) -> B(x1,x2))))) <-> (forall x1. (set(x1) -> (forall x2. (set(x2) -> B(x1,x2))))))
schema regroup3 [B(3)]: ((forall x1. (forall x2. (forall x3. (set(x1) -> (set(x2) -> (set(x3) -> B(x1,x2,x3))))))) <-> (forall x1. (set(x1) -> (forall x2. (set(x2) -> (forall x3. (set(x3) -> B(x1,x2,x3))))))))
schema regroup4 [B(4)]: ((forall x1. (forall x2. (forall x3. (forall x4. (set(x1) -> (set(x2) -> (set(x3) -> (set(x4) -> B(x1,x2,x3,x4))))))))) <-> (forall x1. (set(x1) -> (forall x2. (set(x2) -> (forall x3. (set(x3) -> (forall x4. (set(x4) -> B(x1,x2,x3,x4))))))))))
schema regroup5 [B(5)]: ((forall x1. (forall x2. (forall x3. (forall x4. (forall x5. (set(x1) -> (set(x2) -> (set(x3) -> (set(x4) -> (set(x5) -> B(x1,x2,x3,x4,x5))))))))))) <-> (forall x1. (set(x1) -> (forall x2. (set(x2) -> (forall x3. (set(x3) -> (forall x4. (set(x4) -> (forall x5. (set(x5) -> B(x1,x2,x3,x4,x5))))))))))))
